WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:01.173
(light music)

00:00:01.173 --> 00:00:02.006
- [Narrator] On your way to the office,

00:00:02.006 --> 00:00:03.930
a message comes in from your boss.

00:00:03.930 --> 00:00:05.523
Can you grab them a coffee?

00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:08.910
They pay you back in a different app.

00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:10.530
You go to another app

00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:12.360
to place an order for pickup

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:15.531
and your mobile wallet
needs to be updated.

00:00:15.531 --> 00:00:17.610
(camera shutters)
Okay, there.

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:19.230
Now coffee,

00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:22.050
which took four apps to accomplish.

00:00:22.050 --> 00:00:25.050
But in China, it takes just one.

00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:26.730
WeChat, a super app.

00:00:26.730 --> 00:00:29.310
- You just don't have to
futz around with your phone,

00:00:29.310 --> 00:00:30.990
remembering different passwords,

00:00:30.990 --> 00:00:32.640
opening and closing different apps.

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:34.320
It just makes it easy.

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:37.320
- [Narrator] So why isn't
there a super app in the US?

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:38.153
- My idea would be like,

00:00:38.153 --> 00:00:39.887
how about if we just copy WeChat?

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:42.810
- [Narrator] Though some
apps have multiple functions,

00:00:42.810 --> 00:00:44.910
creating a super app in the US

00:00:44.910 --> 00:00:47.520
is the holy grail for some tech CEOs,

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:49.350
and for good reason.

00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:51.363
Take another look at WeChat.

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:55.077
The app launched in 2011 as a social media

00:00:55.077 --> 00:00:56.880
and messaging service.

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:01.050
By the next year, it added a
PayPal-like payment option.

00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:04.050
And in the following years
it would integrate a slew

00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:06.750
of other features,
including games, shopping,

00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:09.540
food delivery, healthcare, and mini apps.

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:12.120
- It really sort of does everything.

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:15.420
In some ways, it's a stand-in
for the internet itself.

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:17.520
- [Narrator] And users like it.

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:21.960
By 2022, WeChat had 1.3
billion active users

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:24.330
and helped its parent
company bring in a revenue

00:01:24.330 --> 00:01:26.580
of about $81 billion.

00:01:26.580 --> 00:01:28.320
- The beauty of a super app is that

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:30.030
you're able to move seamlessly

00:01:30.030 --> 00:01:31.530
between different functions,

00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:33.570
share information and data

00:01:33.570 --> 00:01:35.550
between the different
parts of that super app.

00:01:35.550 --> 00:01:37.290
That's what makes it work.

00:01:37.290 --> 00:01:40.800
- [Narrator] And that data is
the big draw for companies.

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:42.420
Let's look back at the apps you used

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:44.640
to make that coffee order again.

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:47.130
Apps in the US are largely restricted

00:01:47.130 --> 00:01:48.960
from tracking you across your phone.

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:51.360
So each company is only collecting data

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:53.160
while their app is open.

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:55.320
But with WeChat, you
don't have to leave it

00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:57.720
to make that order or message your boss,

00:01:57.720 --> 00:02:01.140
or even post a photo of
that coffee on social media.

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:04.650
That one company gets
your data for all of it.

00:02:04.650 --> 00:02:06.870
- Having a better
understanding of what people

00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:09.270
are purchasing when they're
purchasing those things,

00:02:09.270 --> 00:02:13.230
you know, that becomes
invaluable for a business.

00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:17.850
Or if you're a government
for, you know, tax purposes.

00:02:17.850 --> 00:02:19.350
- [Narrator] That's one of the reasons

00:02:19.350 --> 00:02:22.260
why companies from multiple
industries in the US

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:25.230
have made attempts at
creating a super app,

00:02:25.230 --> 00:02:28.290
but their growth has been
slowed by several things.

00:02:28.290 --> 00:02:29.850
A super app in the US

00:02:29.850 --> 00:02:33.420
would have to contend against
thousands of other apps,

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:36.630
and changing consumer
behavior can be difficult.

00:02:36.630 --> 00:02:39.660
- We're not really used to
using one app for everything.

00:02:39.660 --> 00:02:43.230
And that's why when companies
like Facebook in the past

00:02:43.230 --> 00:02:45.990
have tried to launch singular apps

00:02:45.990 --> 00:02:48.150
that do more than one thing at a time,

00:02:48.150 --> 00:02:50.250
consumers have largely rejected them.

00:02:50.250 --> 00:02:52.620
- [Narrator] Facebook Messenger
said it would simplify

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:56.880
its app back in 2018 after
adding features like games.

00:02:56.880 --> 00:02:59.040
Its head, at the time, said that the app

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:01.320
had become too cluttered.

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:04.980
Another key issue is
incorporating a payment system.

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:06.600
Take Snap for example.

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:10.110
Its payment system, Snapcash,
shut down after four years,

00:03:10.110 --> 00:03:13.440
while apps like Venmo
and Zelle are growing.

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:17.550
- If you can integrate that
payment system into a super app,

00:03:17.550 --> 00:03:20.190
it just makes that app much more sticky.

00:03:20.190 --> 00:03:23.070
But it also means, in most cases,

00:03:23.070 --> 00:03:25.980
that you can control
the transaction costs.

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:28.890
Maybe you're making a better
margin in the transaction

00:03:28.890 --> 00:03:31.110
and as your user base grows,

00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:33.180
you start to scale.

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:35.340
- [Narrator] But integrating
that payment system

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:37.650
is a big lift in the US.

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:39.390
- A lot of tech companies aren't also

00:03:39.390 --> 00:03:41.820
a financial services company
right out of the box.

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:42.653
It's complicated.

00:03:42.653 --> 00:03:44.280
There's regulations involved.

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:47.400
There's different international
rules and things like that.

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:49.140
- [Narrator] Facebook's
attempt at integrating

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:51.720
a crypto payment system failed in part

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:54.180
because lawmakers and
regulators were concerned

00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:57.240
about payments being used
for illicit activity.

00:03:57.240 --> 00:03:59.100
The Consumer Financial Protection Bureau

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:01.350
has cracked down on big tech platforms

00:04:01.350 --> 00:04:04.080
using payment systems in recent years.

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:06.840
And data privacy can also become a concern

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:09.120
for consumers and lawmakers.

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:12.150
- Right now, a lot of people distrust

00:04:12.150 --> 00:04:13.650
the big tech companies,

00:04:13.650 --> 00:04:16.860
and the idea of a single tech company,

00:04:16.860 --> 00:04:20.490
like a Meta or an Amazon or a Google,

00:04:20.490 --> 00:04:23.040
offering us an app that really houses

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:24.660
so many different things

00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:27.460
from online payments to bill management

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:30.930
to health records, you know,

00:04:30.930 --> 00:04:32.970
is kinda scary to people.

00:04:32.970 --> 00:04:35.310
- [Narrator] And that becomes
an issue for companies.

00:04:35.310 --> 00:04:36.720
- I don't think at this point

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:39.030
technology companies are interested

00:04:39.030 --> 00:04:42.000
in making it look like
they are consolidating

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:44.790
their control over our digital lives

00:04:44.790 --> 00:04:45.990
any more than they already have.

00:04:45.990 --> 00:04:48.600
I mean, that would just
invite regulatory scrutiny.

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:50.340
- [Narrator] Some experts and analysts

00:04:50.340 --> 00:04:52.830
think the future for super apps in the US

00:04:52.830 --> 00:04:55.320
may look different than WeChat.

00:04:55.320 --> 00:04:56.970
- I think the path forward

00:04:56.970 --> 00:04:59.910
for the super app concept
in the United States

00:04:59.910 --> 00:05:01.740
has more to do with verticals

00:05:01.740 --> 00:05:04.110
than sort of a one ring to rule them all.

00:05:04.110 --> 00:05:06.720
- [Narrator] So for example,
a single healthcare app

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:08.610
might house your medical information,

00:05:08.610 --> 00:05:12.180
as well as features for making
appointments and payments.

00:05:12.180 --> 00:05:13.950
- [Narrator] The first
champion that emerges

00:05:13.950 --> 00:05:18.950
and creates a seamless,
frictionless super app-like program

00:05:19.500 --> 00:05:22.650
that allowed doctors
to plug in their data,

00:05:22.650 --> 00:05:24.720
you know, electronic health records stuff,

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:28.650
I think that that is gonna
be a huge, huge game changer.

00:05:28.650 --> 00:05:30.606
A massive disruptor.

00:05:30.606 --> 00:05:33.189
(upbeat music)

